WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.519
The next item on the agenda, item nine, is
the NHS

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:38.040
Performance Report. Barbara, thank you very
much for this report, and can I commend the

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.579
succinctness and the focus of the report and
invite you to comment on it?

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:51.510
Thank you, Chairman. I would like to highlight
a couple of issues in the report and expand

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:57.789
on them for the Board, and I would also like
to mention one other issue alongside that.

00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:03.489
I would like to highlight the outcome of the
planning and contracting round, where NHS

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:09.110
England teams have been supporting our CCGs
to make sure that the total amount of activity

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:17.140
commissioned for our patients was adequate.
It is absolutely vital that our provider services

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:23.170
can make the right plans in order to put services
in place for patients, and as a result of

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.679
a lot of hard work on everybody’s part we
are now offering contracts where there are

00:01:28.679 --> 00:01:36.950
6.8 million extra elective spells, which is
an increase of 2.7% over the previous year,

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:44.090
and an increase of 5.6 million non-elective
spells, which is an increase of 2.3%. Those

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.810
are being offered in contracts to providers
and, for the most part, all the capacity that

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:54.759
is required has been secured.
This is not to say that it is not our ambition

00:01:54.759 --> 00:02:00.090
to reduce some of this activity which we all
believe is unnecessary, and where it would

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:04.590
be better for patients to have more comprehensive
services at home, in the community, in the

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:10.149
primary care setting, earlier in the course
of the disease in order to reduce the need

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:15.410
for that admission; particularly, I think,
with non-elective admissions. So running alongside

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:23.240
that, every area has through the BCF an ambition
to reduce particularly the elective activity

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:28.340
through a range of interventions across health
and social care, and hopefully those ambitions

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:37.480
will see that this activity is not all found
to be necessary as we go through the year.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:42.400
Where places have not been able to secure
the activity from providers, where there are

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.220
capacity problems, we continue to seek that
capacity. It is a very small number – a

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.720
small number of places, a small number of
specialities – and working with other NHS

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.180
and independent sector providers to put that
in place. The next steps really are looking

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:01.090
at the contingency, because whilst we would
be optimistic that every bit of activity would

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:07.230
be delivered across the country, we know that
certain things have an impact on providers’

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:14.150
ability to deliver all the interventions necessary.
So we have in place a comprehensive PMO across

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:21.040
England which is identifying all the available
additional capacity, both within the NHS and

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.580
within the independent sector, which we could
draw on in any instance where we see that

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:32.360
the commissioned activity is not being delivered.
Plainly, if we see that the commissioned activity

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.670
is being outstripped and CCGs have not commissioned
sufficient activity, then through our local

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.390
offices we will need to go back round the
loop with them and make sure that, very quickly

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:47.420
in the year, they commission the extra activity
so that providers can put things in place.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:53.110
The second area I would like to highlight
is cancer, particularly on the back of the

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.620
last conversations that we have had. I think
it is important to remind everyone that nine

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:03.480
out of ten of the cancer standards are met,
but we are falling behind in the 62-day cancer

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:09.420
standard. This is for good reasons, not for
bad reasons; it is not that the NHS is not

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.750
delivering lots of activity and lots of care
for patients, it is because, because of some

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.609
of the interventions, because of awareness
and patients coming forward earlier and a

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:22.599
lot of the campaigns we have had, more and
more patients are presenting who might have

00:04:22.599 --> 00:04:26.689
cancer, so there is more to deal with, and
what has happened is we are treating more

00:04:26.689 --> 00:04:31.680
patients, but not enough.
We have a number of immediate steps in place

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.629
and some longer-term steps in place; many
of the longer-term ones, of course, will be

00:04:36.629 --> 00:04:40.749
aligned with what goes on in the Cancer Plan,
but just to let the Board be aware that we

00:04:40.749 --> 00:04:49.669
are working very, very closely with TDA and
Monitor. We have a plan to address the failings

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:54.419
in the cancer standards, a comprehensive plan
across a range of issues; the most important

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:59.279
one probably being to put more endoscopy capacity
into the system, because there is really good

00:04:59.279 --> 00:05:04.300
evidence that this is where one of the biggest
blocks is. So again, we are assessing the

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:08.629
total amount of endoscopy capacity that is
available across the country, because often

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:13.719
there are mobile units who can deliver this
service, so it is not about having to persuade

00:05:13.719 --> 00:05:16.909
patients to travel long distances or away
from providers.

00:05:16.909 --> 00:05:24.270
Our short-term focus is on a number of flow
issues within trusts. As I say, we are working

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.379
closely with Monitor and TDA with this, and
on making sure that we have the right endoscopy

00:05:28.379 --> 00:05:29.620
capacity where necessary. For now, our heavy
focus immediately is on those areas where

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:30.559
there obviously is not enough capacity and
they are not delivering the standard, but

00:05:30.559 --> 00:05:35.809
in the more medium term we will be working
with our tripartite partners and with every

00:05:35.809 --> 00:05:39.039
area across England to make sure that the
capacity planning for the increase which we

00:05:39.039 --> 00:05:45.400
expect to see – we have already seen an
increase, we expect it to become greater – we

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:55.559
are going to be working to make sure that
they have the right capacity plans in place

00:05:55.559 --> 00:05:58.879
to see us through future years, not just for
this year.

00:05:58.879 --> 00:06:04.860
Those were my two things to highlight within
the report. I would just like, if I can, to

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:10.719
give an update on exercise Strong Tower; part
of our emergency planning exercise which,

00:06:10.719 --> 00:06:15.490
for obvious reasons, could not be in the written
report at the time it came out. The Board

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:21.919
will be aware of NHS England’s role in the
event of a serious emergency; the role changes

00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:29.029
completely to one where we have complete responsibility
for ensuring that the whole of the NHS acts

00:06:29.029 --> 00:06:35.240
in concert and acts appropriately. We do do
a huge number of exercises in the emergency

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.650
planning team with the NHS and within NHS
England, at local, regional and national level,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.580
to make sure we are prepared, but it is rare
that there is such a big exercise as Strong

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:54.610
Tower, which was a tier-one exercise initiated
by the Home Office as part of the terrorist

00:06:54.610 --> 00:07:03.149
response. The exercise was about a marauding
firearms attack and terrorist siege. We took

00:07:03.149 --> 00:07:07.800
part obviously to make sure that there were
two things in place. Firstly, that we as NHS

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.759
England could play our part in a very complex
system when something like this happens, but

00:07:12.759 --> 00:07:17.849
also that we could make sure that the response
in the NHS was absolutely right. It was a

00:07:17.849 --> 00:07:23.740
two-day exercise, as I say, a tier-one exercise
with COBRA, and there was ministerial involvement

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:29.409
in it as well. The main things for us were
standing up the full Instant Response Team

00:07:29.409 --> 00:07:33.619
nationally and standing up the full Instant
Response Team in London, because plainly,

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:37.399
when these kind of things happen there is
an assumption that the height of the problem

00:07:37.399 --> 00:07:43.039
will be in London, so our London team have
a very significant role to play in this.

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:50.210
The early ‘hot’ debrief from Strong Tower
was that the NHS stood up very well, and that

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:54.979
for the most part, everything was in good
place. What I would like to do perhaps is

00:07:54.979 --> 00:08:01.259
to bring back to the Board for the next meeting
a full written report once we have had the

00:08:01.259 --> 00:08:06.339
‘cold’ debrief, and work out all the lessons
we have learnt and what changes we will be

00:08:06.339 --> 00:08:10.050
making on the basis on this. However, I can
just assure the Board that on the basis of

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:14.460
this there was nothing major from a hot debrief
that we would say needs to be done immediately,

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.080
and our processes appear to stand up well.
Thank you very much, Barbara, for that report

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:25.800
and for the Strong Tower report.
It is a very clear report, thank you. On the

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:33.870
ambulance service, I am curious about the
ambulatory response to mental health crisis,

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:39.260
which is something I have been concerned about
for some time. You mentioned the paramedic

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:45.519
training, and I just wondered whether the
mental health response has been included in

00:08:45.519 --> 00:08:51.069
that training? Similarly, the ambulatory response
to mental health crisis, which has often been

00:08:51.069 --> 00:08:54.870
lacking. What happens is that someone in crisis,
particularly if they look like me, is more

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:59.529
likely to get a police response, which is
frankly risky, than an ambulatory one, and

00:08:59.529 --> 00:09:02.500
I just wondered whether that was part of your
thinking or is going to be?

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:09.680
Well, two answers to that. There is certainly
work that goes on with the ambulance services,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:15.589
which NHS England teams are involved in, to
improve the response in mental health crisis

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:18.850
and patients with mental health issues to
make sure that they are taken to the right

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.130
place, there is a good ambulance response,
and that they get the appropriate treatment.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.569
Also, we are doing a lot of work with A&amp;E
departments to try and ensure that there is

00:09:26.569 --> 00:09:30.470
good access to mental health professionals
within A&amp;E departments once they get there.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:35.550
I do not know the answer to the training of
staff. This was particularly about where there

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.690
is a national shortage of paramedics, and
a couple of our ambulance services have had

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:46.709
to seek paramedics coming in from abroad;
London Ambulance Service, for example, already

00:09:46.709 --> 00:09:51.910
has about 150 paramedics in from Australia,
and will be recruiting more, but I will happily

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:58.199
take back. We are working with Health Education
England firstly to increase the recruitment

00:09:58.199 --> 00:10:03.720
of paramedics from the UK in the future, but
I will take that back and ask about the training

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:10.060
of paramedics on mental health issues.
Thank you very much, Barbara, for your report.

